#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572279} :activity/id #uuid "bba7dd75-0519-5756-8558-53f530eff737" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048584 :topic/id #uuid "abbdc596-7744-5a68-9999-a74874659d19" :topic/math-academy-id 2018} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572208 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f96ed3-70f8-5ddc-acb6-71587e72f101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324543} {:answer/id #uuid "71e88fc2-600f-5365-8aff-acc2b54dc6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324914} {:answer/id #uuid "90374c3c-03f1-5264-a569-174dfb35f325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325196} {:answer/id #uuid "aa418aef-76d7-5108-ab92-460aa075c2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325420} {:answer/id #uuid "f69e55ee-410c-52db-978f-37052ef96cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326143}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324543} :answer-field/id #uuid "05c656fc-b22b-5e23-846d-595499735480" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53d94693-fca2-5635-a557-72030e989666" :question/math-academy-id "q-39161" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1+6\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=1-6\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "15c2ddcb-4a6a-5d10-92a7-7eac543f5050" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e4b3e5e-e60f-54f8-a180-20a2dffb4c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571727} {:answer/id #uuid "73a0f75c-e27c-550f-b2be-3fd75e2e5ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571736} {:answer/id #uuid "760f31e6-7cca-54b7-8d98-f5aff61070ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571740} {:answer/id #uuid "dc888f95-a5ef-55ce-b32e-8bfff1554337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571933} {:answer/id #uuid "ee82d2f9-4196-5c21-b0cc-b2a8e520706a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571727} :answer-field/id #uuid "032ad5ea-b156-5e33-a7be-bc141e7f1c34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d87763f-3e1e-5f56-b2a4-bc97bccd7687" :question/math-academy-id "q-140502" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2-5\\text{i},$ and that $A=PC{P}^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2-5\\text{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2-5\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2-5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5+5\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -10 & 5+5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-5+5\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -10{x}_{1}+(5+5\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=2$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "66c2fbfa-b746-5325-80be-b35737eb36eb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572245 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27fcac0c-32b4-50b7-a320-2ac89d925ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571606} {:answer/id #uuid "39c17d8b-7383-565d-82ff-9034e842313a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571643} {:answer/id #uuid "894dd0ee-996e-5946-926e-1667d9661d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571774} {:answer/id #uuid "a5514041-c574-508e-9c48-296d90937fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571825} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a5c562-7c09-52ed-9124-62137601a6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571606} :answer-field/id #uuid "621d2560-2a88-546c-aa01-9273b93c0401" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "80ffb1ef-2b1d-5387-91a1-65dcd637622b" :question/math-academy-id "q-99410" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-5\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2+5\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-4\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7e9b9df9-0488-568f-a29f-8082f11cab44" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572239}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096e7527-4c01-5dce-8ff8-e0db3c75370d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410832} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8fe61d-12cc-5e71-bf5f-8ade179fac6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410834} {:answer/id #uuid "1c87451a-2d1c-5373-8af0-e4594174b876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410836} {:answer/id #uuid "5113c383-2506-5331-9225-ebb1f5af24a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410840} {:answer/id #uuid "f16edcb8-7daa-5bb4-b079-ab44c7accef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410850} :answer-field/id #uuid "8400b796-2f04-536c-986d-13f15ae5b122" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d6880ebc-9daa-5cd0-b5d7-2100fa039d06" :question/math-academy-id "q-99345" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ed8df2a4-c822-5ea5-aa21-e1c6c89fb344" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572245}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e0e53bd-8745-5da4-abb6-0168c5f3b7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392268} {:answer/id #uuid "476bc4f8-15ca-5f43-b421-6be5db9f13ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392269} {:answer/id #uuid "1e73dd85-d5f3-51a5-becd-e240b1df303b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392270} {:answer/id #uuid "67c66a85-607a-5c01-b5d7-7cb5992959b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392271} {:answer/id #uuid "2b845846-0d25-558e-832d-645c178ab7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392272}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392271} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d04520-0124-5203-a746-6e09d2550e03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3f1bb626-14db-51ca-a15c-04bb2468d62d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39124" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+2\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f872b89c-4ebf-575b-acff-0457891853a1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572208}}] :activity/title "Reducing Real 2x2 Matrices to Rotation-Scaling Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571534}]}
